// Code generated by `fitness-release checks`; DO NOT EDIT.

package main

// topics are the `fitness docs` topics, each the doc comments of the
// package whose doc comment names it with a //fitness:topic line.
var topics = []topic{
	{name: "config", pkg: "internal/conf", summary: "Package conf loads .fitnessrc.json — the Go runner's configuration file.", body: "Package conf loads .fitnessrc.json — the Go runner's configuration file. Same keys as the TypeScript FitnessConfig; JSON because the standard library parses it and a config file must not require a toolchain.\n\n## DefaultMaxListItems\n\nDefaultMaxListItems is the longest list prose-budget allows, and the most bullets changelog-bullets allows in a release section, unless proseBudget.maxListItems sets another limit.\n\n## FileName\n\nFileName is the config file the runner reads at the repo root.\n\n## SchemaURL\n\nSchemaURL is the JSON Schema dialect the schema follows.\n\n## Defaults\n\nDefaults holds the value each Config key takes when the config file leaves it unset or, for a number, at zero or below.\n\n## Config\n\nConfig is the shape of .fitnessrc.json, the optional file at a repo's root that tunes which checks run and how strictly they judge.\n\nFields:\n\n- `policy` selects org or external use; both default to all checks. External lets a repo pick, replace, and turn off checks; org runs every check as the shared bar.\n- `checks` optionally selects checks in external mode. Org ignores it.\n- `timeoutMs` replaces the default per-check budget for checks that declare none of their own.\n- `localChecks` names a repo directory of the repo's own check programs, searched before the bundled ones. See ADR 0009.\n- `disabledChecks` names checks external policy leaves out; org policy refuses it. See ADR 0009.\n- `repeatedStringLiterals` holds options for that check.\n- `repeatedStringLiterals.allow` lists exact values the check never flags, for the rare repeat no shared constant can fix. Keep it a shrinking list.\n- `textReadability` holds alarm bands for the text-readability check.\n- `textReadability.maxGrade` is the Coleman-Liau and ARI grade at which a file alarms.\n- `textReadability.maxLix` is the LIX score at which a file alarms.\n- `textReadability.minWords` is the fewest prose words a file needs to be judged.\n- `proseBudget` holds the prose-budget check's hard caps. Each limit falls back to a built-in default when zero.\n- `proseBudget.maxSentenceWords` is the most words one sentence may hold.\n- `proseBudget.maxParagraphSentences` is the most sentences one paragraph may hold.\n- `proseBudget.maxSectionParagraphs` is the most paragraphs one section may hold.\n- `proseBudget.maxListItemWords` is the most words one list item may hold.\n- `proseBudget.maxListItems` is the most items one list may hold, and the most bullets changelog-bullets allows in a release section.\n- `proseBudget.maxWords` is the most prose words one section may hold.\n- `comment` holds comment-structure's budget for an issue or PR comment. Each limit falls back to a built-in default when zero.\n- `comment.maxWords` is the most words one comment may hold.\n- `comment.maxTables` is the most tables one comment may hold.\n\n## Load\n\nLoad reads root's config. A missing file returns (nil, nil): the caller falls back to the defaults. A config that still sets a retired exclusion key returns an error naming each one.\n\n## Config.LocalCheckBinaries\n\nLocalCheckBinaries maps each check in the repo's localChecks directory to its program, by check name. A config without localChecks returns nil. The directory must sit inside root and hold at least one check; every fitness-check-\\<name> entry in it must be an executable file. Other files in the directory are left alone.\n\n## Key\n\nKey is one key the config file may set, as the registered sections declare it: its JSON path, its JSON type, the doc comment on its Go field, and its default.\n\nFields:\n\n- `key` is the key's dotted JSON path, such as proseBudget.maxWords.\n- `type` is the key's JSON type: string, integer, number, boolean, array, or object.\n- `items` is the JSON type of an array's items.\n- `enum` lists the values a string key accepts, from its enum tag.\n- `description` is the doc comment on the key's Go field.\n- `default` is the value the key takes when the file leaves it unset, or nil for none.\n- `keys` are an object's own keys.\n\n## Keys\n\nKeys lists every key the registered sections declare, in registration and declaration order, with the doc of the type that holds them.\n\n## Section\n\nSection is a registered package's keys in the config file. Loading keys takes a Section, and only Register makes one, so no code can read keys the schema does not describe.\n\n## Register\n\nRegister declares the keys a package reads from the config file: their Go type T, a T holding each key's default, and the Go source that declares T. The schema, `fitness config`, and its descriptions all come from these. A package keeps the returned Section in a package variable and loads its keys with it. A key that another section already declares panics, since one key must mean one thing.\n\n## Section.Load\n\nLoad reads root's config into a T, which holds the keys a caller owns, such as a language module's options. It returns (nil, nil) without a config file and fails as the package's Load does.\n\n## Setting\n\nSetting is one key's value in a repo.\n\nFields:\n\n- `key` is the key's dotted JSON path.\n- `value` is what the key holds: the file's value, else its default.\n- `source` says where Value came from: file, default, or unset.\n- `description` is the doc comment on the key's Go field.\n\n## Settings\n\nSettings lists each key in root's config file with its value and where that came from, in Keys order, objects flattened to their keys. An invalid file fails as Load does.\n\n## MaxListItems\n\nMaxListItems is the repo's list limit: proseBudget.maxListItems when set, else DefaultMaxListItems.\n\n## Schema\n\nSchema is a JSON Schema for the config file, built from the registered sections: one property per key, each with its type, description, and default. A key no section declares fails it, except `$schema`, which names the schema for an editor.\n"},
	{name: "coverage", pkg: "internal/goal", summary: "Package goal holds the coverage goal every coverage check judges by, whichever language it measures: every counted line, branch or statement covered.", body: "Package goal holds the coverage goal every coverage check judges by, whichever language it measures: every counted line, branch or statement covered. A check compares exact counts, so a total that rounds to the goal still falls short.\n\n## Percent\n\nPercent is the coverage goal, as a percentage.\n\n## Met\n\nMet reports whether covered of total meets the goal. Nothing to cover meets it.\n\n## Text\n\nText is the goal as findings print it, such as \"100%\".\n"},
	{name: "github", pkg: "internal/metadata", summary: "Package metadata shares GitHub judgments between local checks and Actions.", body: "Package metadata shares GitHub judgments between local checks and Actions.\n\nIn GitHub Actions, `fitness pr-check` judges a pull request's title, body, closed issues and changelog link. `fitness issue-check` judges an issue by its kind label, and `fitness plan-check` judges Plan issues only. `fitness close-check` reopens an issue closed with unchecked items, `fitness dupe-check` notes an issue that reads like an older one, and `fitness comment-check` replies once to a comment that breaks the comment rules. Each reads the Actions event, runs the body checks on the text, and then its after-effect comments, labels or reopens.\n\nLocally, `fitness branch-check` runs the same judgments on this branch's open PR and the issues it closes, without posting anything, and the pre-push hook runs it before a push.\n\n## PushDeadline\n\nPushDeadline bounds every GitHub read one push makes, the gate's and local-branch's together, and any one GitHub read outside a push.\n\n## CommentChecks\n\nCommentChecks judge one issue or PR comment: its own structure, then the prose and markdown checks every Plan and PR body gets.\n\n## KnownBots\n\nKnownBots are the org's automation accounts, whose comments no one can fix by hand: the Actions token, the org App, Release Please, and Dependabot. This is the one list; FITNESS\\_COMMENT\\_IGNORE\\_AUTHORS replaces it for a caller that needs another. The comment-check workflows' job conditions repeat it, so a bot comment starts no runner; change both.\n\n## RepoChecks\n\nRepoChecks applies a repo's own check settings to the GitHub commands: Find returns a check's program and Off names a check the repo turns off. The fitness executable sets Find to its built-in checks when it starts, and to the repo's checks from .fitnessrc.json before RunGH. Left nil, no check runs, so each one fails closed with no result.\n\n## PushPlans\n\nPushPlans are the Plans one push read successfully, each read once. A failed read is not kept, so a later ask tries GitHub again while the push's deadline lasts.\n\n## NewPushPlans\n\nNewPushPlans starts a push with no Plans read, whose reads all end when ctx, the push's deadline from PushContext, does.\n\n## PushPlans.Approved\n\nApproved reports whether issue n carries the exact `Plan` label and an approval comment, reading it from GitHub until a read succeeds.\n\n## PushPlans.Handoff\n\nHandoff writes the reads to a file only this user can read or write and names it for local-branch. The returned func removes the file.\n\n## PushPlans.TimedOut\n\nTimedOut is one line naming the GitHub host and the deadline once the push's deadline has passed, else empty.\n\n## GitHubAPI\n\nGitHubAPI is the address of GitHub's API: GITHUB\\_API\\_URL, which Actions and the release lookup use, else api.github.com for github.com, else GitHubHost itself, where GitHub Enterprise Server serves its API.\n\n## GitHubHost\n\nGitHubHost is the host gh reads from: GH\\_HOST, else github.com.\n\n## LocalBranch\n\nLocalBranch judges live metadata through the normal check result protocol.\n\n## PushContext\n\nPushContext opens the deadline every GitHub read of one push shares.\n\n## RunGH\n\nRunGH runs the named GitHub command against the Actions event.\n\n## UnreachableGitHub\n\nUnreachableGitHub probes GitHub's API host, once per process however often it is asked, and returns the host when it did not answer, else empty. The pre-push gate and the runner share the one answer.\n\n## WriteBody\n\nWriteBody saves body to a temp file for the --body-file checks; the caller removes it.\n"},
	{name: "hooks", pkg: "internal/hooks", summary: "Package hooks embeds the canonical may-journal git hooks that `fitness init` installs into a consumer repo.", body: "Package hooks embeds the canonical may-journal git hooks that `fitness init` installs into a consumer repo. The hooks run the latest fitness through the installer in $FITNESS\\_INSTALL\\_DIR (default ~/.local/share/fitness), so no fitness on the PATH is needed.\n\n## FS\n\nFS holds the hook scripts under files/.\n\n## Names\n\nNames lists the installed hook filenames, in no particular order.\n\n## Read\n\nRead returns the embedded script for one hook name.\n"},
	{name: "languages", pkg: "internal/lang", summary: "Package lang defines what a language module brings to a run, and orders what the modules claim against what is installed or embedded.", body: "Package lang defines what a language module brings to a run, and orders what the modules claim against what is installed or embedded. A module names how to spot its language, the files it reads and writes, the tools it runs, its checks, its spell dictionaries, the words that name it as a technology, and how it proves a runtime. Everything no module claims is repo-wide, so a new repo-wide check or dictionary needs no list entry. The modules themselves live in the langs package, and each language's own code in a folder named for it beside that package.\n\n## Module\n\nModule is one language's entry in the registry.\n\nFields:\n\n- `Name` labels the module.\n- `Detect` reports whether the repo at root uses the module's language.\n- `Exts` are the source file endings the language owns.\n- `Manifests` are the file names that mark a project of the language.\n- `Lockfiles` are the file names its package managers write on install, each a name or a path.Match pattern for one.\n- `Wrappers` are the names of the files its build tools' wrappers write, and a repo commits as written, to pin the tool's version.\n- `BuildOutput`: BuildOutput, when set, lists the tracked files in a repo that are build output the language's projects write, which git must not track.\n- `Tools` are the programs its checks run.\n- `Checks` are the check names the module claims.\n- `Dicts` are the embedded spell dictionary files the module claims.\n- `Technology` is the name the language's runtime products give their technology.\n- `TechnologyWords` are the lowercase words an architecture container's technology string may use to name the language.\n- `Runtime` reads the language's manifests and sources for the runtimes a repo ships.\n- `Views` reads the language's sources for the architecture views: declarations and relations for the code view, and, when set, the references that prove the components view's edges.\n- `Inventory` are the source endings the architecture views list as the language's sources.\n- `Tests` finds the language's requirement tests.\n- `Scans` are the source endings a repo-wide check reads in the language, by check name.\n- `ToolConfig`: ToolConfig, when set, reports a source file that configures a tool, such as a bundler config, rather than product code.\n- `TestFile`: TestFile, when set, reports a file the language's test runner finds by its name or folder.\n\n## Testing\n\nTesting returns the modules that find requirement tests, in registry order.\n\n## Test\n\nTest is one test declaration and the acceptance ID its name carries, if any.\n\nFields:\n\n- `Name` is the test's name as declared.\n- `ID` is the acceptance ID the name carries, or \"\" for none.\n- `Loc` is where the test is declared, as file:line.\n- `File` is the path of the file declaring the test.\n\n## TestPrefix\n\nTestPrefix is how a module whose test names carry IDs names them: the prefix, then the acceptance ID with \\_ for its dot, so Test0001\\_1 or test0001\\_1 owns 0001.1.\n\n## TestPrefix.ID\n\nID returns the acceptance ID a test name carries, or \"\".\n\n## TestPrefix.Name\n\nName is the test name that owns id.\n\n## Tests\n\nTests is how a module finds its requirement tests.\n\nFields:\n\n- `Scan`: Scan, when set, reads the repo's tests and the files holding them, and reports whether the repo is written in the language. A module without it finds no requirement tests yet.\n- `Name` is the test name that owns an acceptance ID, such as Test0001\\_1.\n- `Source`: Source, when set, reports a source path, as tracked, only tests build, which the architecture views class as a test. It gets the path's own case, since a language may tell folders apart by it.\n- `Numbers`: Numbers, when set, lists the requirement numbers a test file's name carries, or none for a file not named for requirements.\n- `Tools`: Tools, when set, judges how the repo's tests are written and run, given the files Scan found tests in. It returns one error per test file or run that uses a tool the language's requirement tests may not use.\n- `Reads` reports a tracked file Scan or Tools may read, so a copy of the repo made only to judge its tests can hold just those files.\n\n## BuildOutput\n\nBuildOutput returns the tracked build output files every module finds in the repo at root, in registry order.\n\n## Catalog\n\nCatalog orders the default checks: the installed checks no module claims and no ignore file names, sorted, then each module's claimed checks, sorted, in registry order.\n\n## Dicts\n\nDicts orders the dictionary files: the embedded files no module claims, sorted, then each module's claimed files, sorted, in registry order.\n\n## Exts\n\nExts returns every module's source endings, in registry order.\n\n## Ignored\n\nIgnored returns the names in the ignores folder, sorted.\n\n## IgnoredWhy\n\nIgnoredWhy is the reason the ignores folder gives for keeping check out of the default catalog, its comment lines joined, or \"\" when it names no such check.\n\n## Inventory\n\nInventory returns every module's inventory endings, in registry order.\n\n## IsLockfile\n\nIsLockfile returns a test, built once, of whether a file's base name is a lockfile a package manager writes, by any module's names or patterns or the lockfiles folder.\n\n## IsTestFile\n\nIsTestFile reports whether any module's test runner finds file by name.\n\n## IsTestSource\n\nIsTestSource reports whether a module classes the source path as built only for tests.\n\n## IsToolConfig\n\nIsToolConfig reports whether the module owning file's ending says it configures a tool.\n\n## IsToolWritten\n\nIsToolWritten returns a test, built once, of whether a file is one a tool writes and a repo commits as written: a lockfile, or a build wrapper file any module names. Its words and repeats are the tool's.\n\n## Lockfiles\n\nLockfiles returns every module's lockfile names, in registry order, then those of the ecosystems no module covers.\n\n## Runtimes\n\nRuntimes returns every module's runtime reader, in registry order.\n\n## ScanExts\n\nScanExts returns every module's source endings for the check, in registry order.\n\n## Sources\n\nSources maps each role to the endings of the languages no module covers that play it, in file name order.\n\n## TechnologyWords\n\nTechnologyWords maps each module's technology name to its words.\n\n## TestFileNumbers\n\nTestFileNumbers lists the requirement numbers a test file's name carries, from the first module that reads any.\n\n## TestReads\n\nTestReads reports whether any module's Tests.Reads keeps file.\n\n## Views\n\nViews returns every module's architecture view reader, in registry order, leaving out modules that read none.\n"},
	{name: "protocol", pkg: "internal/checkkit", summary: "Package checkkit is the contract between the fitness runner and a check binary, plus the main-function scaffolding every check builds on.", body: "Package checkkit is the contract between the fitness runner and a check binary, plus the main-function scaffolding every check builds on.\n\nProtocol: the runner execs `fitness-check-<name> --root <dir> [args…]` with cwd = root and context in FITNESS\\_\\* environment variables. The check prints exactly one JSON Result on stdout; anything meant for humans (banners, tool passthrough) goes to stderr. Exit code 0 when the check passed and 1 when it found problems. Exit 3 means the check could not judge (a panic or an internal error) and the Result carries that as info, which never fails a run. `--describe` prints a Describe JSON and exits without running.\n\n## ScopeFiles, ScopeBlob, ScopeGit, ScopeGitHub\n\nThe scopes a check declares: what it reads when it judges a repo.\n\n## BinPrefix\n\nBinPrefix starts the name every built-in check runs under, and every local check's file name.\n\n## ExitInternal\n\nExitInternal is the exit code of a check that could not judge the repo.\n\n## NetworkEnv\n\nNetworkEnv names the variable through which the runner tells each check that GitHub's API host did not answer its probe: `unreachable:<host>`. Unset means the host answered or the run probed nothing.\n\n## ProbeTimeout\n\nProbeTimeout bounds the runner's one probe of GitHub's API host.\n\n## ScopeFileEnv\n\nScopeFileEnv names the file, one path per line, that lists the files under a `fitness --dir` folder. A folder can list more paths than one variable holds, so the runner hands them over in a file.\n\n## Check\n\nCheck is one check implementation: its metadata and run function.\n\nFields:\n\n- `Describe` is the metadata --describe prints.\n- `Run` receives the repo root and passthrough args; the returned Result is emitted verbatim. An error return means the check could not judge: it is reported as info with exit 3, never as a finding.\n\n## Describe\n\nDescribe is the metadata a check prints for `--describe`.\n\nFields:\n\n- `name` is the check name, as `fitness <name>` runs it.\n- `summary` says in one line what the check enforces. A built-in check leaves it empty: its package doc comment's first sentence is generated into the runner, which serves it here.\n- `scope` is one of the Scope constants: what the check reads.\n- `timeoutMs` is the check's execution budget; 0 means the runner default.\n- `contextInlineArg` names a flag (e.g. \"--message\") whose value the runner extracts from single-check argv into FITNESS\\_CTX\\_MESSAGE and strips from passthrough. Empty when the check takes no inline context.\n- `blob` marks a check that judges a text given with `--body-file`, so `fitness --blob` runs it; the rest need files and are skipped there.\n\n## Finding\n\nFinding is one problem a check found.\n\nFields:\n\n- `file` is the path the problem is in, relative to the repo root, or empty when it is not in one file.\n- `line` is the 1-based line in File, or 0 when the whole file is meant.\n- `column` is the 1-based column on Line, or 0 when not known.\n- `rule` names what the check enforces here, in lowercase words joined by dashes, such as unknown-word.\n- `message` says what is wrong, in words a person reads.\n- `fix` says what to change so the check passes, when one change does.\n- `explain` is the command that prints why the check exists. The check's entry point fills it in from the check's name.\n\n## Outcome\n\nOutcome is what one tool run left behind. Code is the exit code, or 1 when the process ended without one, so a caller that only reads Code sees any failure. SpawnErr and Signaled tell a broken environment apart from a tool that ran and judged.\n\nFields:\n\n- `Code` is the exit code, or 1 when the process ended without one.\n- `Stdout` is what the tool printed on standard output.\n- `Stderr` is what the tool printed on standard error.\n- `SpawnErr` says why the tool could not start, nil when it did.\n- `Signaled` is true when a signal ended the tool.\n- `Err` is the error the run returned, nil on exit 0.\n\n## Exec\n\nExec runs cmd with stdout and stderr captured apart. A writer the caller already set on cmd.Stderr, such as os.Stderr for live output, still receives the stream.\n\n## ExecCombined\n\nExecCombined runs cmd with stdout and stderr in one buffer, in the order the tool wrote them, and returns it as Stdout.\n\n## Outcome.EnvErr\n\nEnvErr is the internal error for a run that never judged: the tool could not start, or a signal ended it. Nil when the tool ran to an exit code.\n\n## Program\n\nProgram is how to start one check.\n\nFields:\n\n- `Path` is the file to exec.\n- `Name`: Name, when set, is the program name the check starts under: the one fitness executable runs the built-in check that name names.\n\n## Builtin\n\nBuiltin returns the program for a built-in check: the running fitness executable, started as fitness-check-\\<name>. Every check runs from that one file, so a new install loads one binary instead of one per check.\n\n## Self\n\nSelf returns the program that starts the running check again: this executable, under the name it was started with, since one fitness executable holds every check and the name picks which one runs.\n\n## Program.Command\n\nCommand returns the command that runs the program with args.\n\n## Result\n\nResult is the single JSON object a check prints on stdout.\n\nFields:\n\n- `ok` is true when the check found no problem.\n- `errors` lists each problem found, one line each.\n- `filesChecked` counts the files judged; -1 when the check judged none.\n- `info` carries notes that never fail a run, such as an internal error.\n- `findings` repeats Errors in a shape an agent can act on without parsing prose: where, which rule, and how to fix it. It is optional, so a check that sets none still speaks the protocol, and Errors stays for the readers that predate it.\n\n## Fail\n\nFail builds a failing Result.\n\n## Found\n\nFound builds a Result from findings: failing with one error line each, rendered by line in the check's own words, or passing with none.\n\n## Info\n\nInfo builds a passing Result that carries info notes.\n\n## Pass\n\nPass builds a passing Result.\n\n## Beside\n\nBeside maps the name of each fitness-check-\\<name> program beside the running fitness executable to its path. An installed bundle links every built-in check there to fitness itself; those links are the built-in checks, so they are left out. Any other program there adds a check, or replaces the built-in check of its name. None is no error, as after `go install`.\n\n## ChangedFiles\n\nChangedFiles returns the paths the runner scoped this run to: the staged files at commit time, a pull request's diff against its base, or the files under a `--dir` folder. Nil means the run is unscoped and every file is judged.\n\n## CtxMessage\n\nCtxMessage returns the context-inline commit message and whether the runner provided one at all (present-but-empty differs from absent).\n\n## DiffFiles\n\nDiffFiles returns the paths a commit or pull request changes. Nil means the run changes nothing, as in a full scan or a `--dir` folder, so a check that pairs changes, such as requirements, judges no pairs.\n\n## Document\n\nDocument sets the summary --describe prints when the check declares none. The runner calls it with the summary generated from the check's package doc comment before it starts a built-in check.\n\n## EnabledChecks\n\nEnabledChecks returns the enabled check names for this run, in order.\n\n## ExplainCommand\n\nExplainCommand is the command that prints why check exists.\n\n## Internal\n\nInternal marks err as an internal error, reported as info with exit 3. A nil err stays nil.\n\n## IsInternal\n\nIsInternal reports whether err, or an error it wraps, is internal.\n\n## LastJSONLine\n\nLastJSONLine returns the last nonempty stdout line after any tool noise.\n\n## Lines\n\nLines renders each finding as an error line with line.\n\n## Main\n\nMain is the entry point every check binary delegates to: it parses --root/--describe, runs the check, and emits the protocol JSON.\n\n## Offline\n\nOffline is the info note for a check that skips its network reads, named by skipped, because the runner's probe found GitHub's API host unreachable; empty when the reads should go ahead.\n\n## OfflineNote\n\nOfflineNote says that host did not answer the probe, through which proxy, and what was skipped without connecting.\n\n## Probe\n\nProbe opens one connection to target's host and closes it, through the proxy the environment names for it, within ProbeTimeout. Through an HTTP or HTTPS proxy it asks the proxy to CONNECT and waits for its answer. A nil error means the host answered. A proxy of another kind, such as SOCKS, counts as answering, so the reads go ahead as before.\n\n## ProxyFor\n\nProxyFor is the proxy HTTPS\\_PROXY, HTTP\\_PROXY and NO\\_PROXY name for target, as Go's HTTP client and gh pick it, or nil for none.\n\n## SandboxText\n\nSandboxText returns the first sandbox phrase in output, matched without case, or empty when there is none.\n\n## StagedFiles\n\nStagedFiles returns the staged paths the runner provided (may be empty).\n\n## ToolMissing\n\nToolMissing is the internal error for a tool the check needs but cannot find.\n\n## Unreachable\n\nUnreachable is the host the runner's probe could not reach, from NetworkEnv, or empty when it answered or was not probed.\n\n## UnreachableEnv\n\nUnreachableEnv is the NetworkEnv entry that names host as unreachable.\n"},
}
